  message: "DONE 2026-09-09. Record, instruments and pre-registration:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/t0_sizing_09092026/kurug/ (PLAN.md written before any\nnumber existed, eligible.py, ADJUDICATION.md, RESULT.md).\n\nANSWER: THE AMBIGUOUS-NAME ROUTE DOES NOT BLIND THESE FOUR. One row across four\nlanguages fails through it.\n\n    lang     repo                 R2 called  reached   control    verdict\n    erlang   rebar3                      14   14 100%   62/63     PASS\n    cpp      modsecurity                  3    2  67%    9/17     PASS (dup row)\n    haskell  servant                      3    1  33%   14/26     1 R2 FAILURE\n    elixir   phoenix-framework           20   11  55%   33/43     FAILS, OTHER CAUSE\n\nINSTRUMENT CALIBRATED BEFORE USE against C on qemu-dtc, where the answer is\nknown in BOTH directions: pre-WI-lajus 0/4 reached, post-fix 3/4, control 9/10\nin both arms. It reproduced that signed movement exactly, and reproduced it\nagain unchanged after two rounds of tightening.\n\nerlang and cpp PASS via the module_hint disjunct -- erlang writes the module at\nthe call site by convention, cpp stamps its `#include` set (WI-rupik/WI-mafik).\nPre-registered prediction HELD for that pair. cpp's single apparent miss is\n`sys/stat.stat` at the SAME call site where `unistd.stat` reaches: a duplicate\ncatalogue row, filed as WI-fovub, not a reach failure.\n\nhaskell is the only genuine R2 failure: `Prelude.print`, 6 bare sites, module\ncontext only in the import list. Filed WI-lokun. The pre-registration predicted\nUNDECIDED on an empty population; it was not empty. REFUTED.\n\nelixir fails for TWO reasons, NEITHER of them this route -- the F3 gate never\nsees an edge to refuse:\n  WI-kafor  a module-qualified call binds by BARE NAME to a first-party symbol.\n            `Logger.error` -> a function in a CODE-GENERATOR TEMPLATE;\n            `Logger.info` -> a **defp** in another module, which Elixir does not\n            permit to be called cross-module at all. Read in code: the guard\n            `any(f\"{module_name}.\" in k ...)` is a SUBSTRING test where its own\n            comment describes a prefix-component test, and phoenix defines\n            `Phoenix.Logger`. WI-zuvik (done) IS this guard; entry filed there.\n  WI-kigub  `:atom.function` calls (`:ets.insert`, `:httpc.request`) emit NO\n            edge -- `_handle_dot_call` returns early when the dot node has no\n            `alias` child. Every OTP module an Elixir project touches is\n            invisible by the same mechanism.\n\nTWO ELIGIBILITY BUGS IN MY OWN INSTRUMENT, both found by reading rows back\nagainst source, both of which CHANGED AN ANSWER:\n  1. A row can be called in a file the ANALYZER NEVER OPENED. rebar3 vendors\n     under `vendor/`, the survey emits 0 edges from there, and all six of\n     erlang's apparent misses were vendored. Gating on the analyzer's own\n     account of what it read moved erlang 6 misses -> 0. One layer further out\n     than this item's own warning about uncalled rows.\n  2. A bare-name regex matches non-calls: a JavaScript FFI string literal\n     `\"$1.send()\"` scored as two socket sends, a local binding `accept = ...`\n     as 13 accepts, Pipes' `P.yield` as Control.Concurrent.yield, and a member\n     DECLARATION `const char *read(size_t *len)` as a read() call.\n\nNOT CLAIMED: the SAFE-row control rates are reported but not adjudicated per\nrow, so no failure count is drawn from them. One repository per language --\nthis SIZES the cells, it does not certify the languages."  # 7f9f
